# Line endings are not a matter of local configuration in this repository.
#
# Several of its trees are pinned by SHA-256, so a checkout that rewrote their
# line endings does not produce a variant of the file — it produces a build that
# refuses to configure. That is not hypothetical: the first CI run on a GitHub
# Windows runner, whose git defaults to core.autocrlf=true, stopped at
# "the bundled variant configuration does not match the SHA-256
# pinned-inputs.json pins". The same rewrite would break
# `shasum -a 256 -c SOURCES.sha256`, which pinned-inputs.json documents as the
# way to verify the vendored snapshots, on every text file in them.

# Stored and checked out with LF on every platform, so a working tree is
# byte-identical everywhere and hashes taken on one machine mean something on
# another. Nothing here is a Windows file format that wants CRLF; when the
# Windows frontend brings one, it gets its own line below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The pinned trees are not normalised at all. Their bytes ARE the pin, so git
# hands them over exactly as they were committed whatever anyone's configuration
# says: the vendored engine and SQLite snapshots against their SOURCES.sha256
# manifests, the variant configuration against pinned-inputs.json, and the
# fixtures against the golden bytes the codec suites compare. `eol` is unset
# here as well as `text`, and not only for tidiness — git documents `eol` as
# enabling conversion "without any content checks, effectively setting the text
# attribute", so leaving it inherited from the line above would leave these
# paths saying two things at once.
core/assets/** -text -eol
core/third_party/** -text -eol
fixtures/** -text -eol
